inical guidance and patient decision-making around contraception and pregnancy planning during and after therapy. [Data: Entities (1509, 869); Relationships (2422, 4445)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Impaired fertility is a recognized risk linked to cancer treatment, requiring proactive discussion\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community explicitly links some cancer treatments to impaired fertility risk, described as damage to body parts needed for reproduction. This elevates fertility preservation and family planning into a consequential part of treatment decision-making. [Data: Entities (5704, 869); Relationships (12771)]\\n\\nThe broader cancer treatment description contextualizes these risks within reproductive implications of therapy (e.g., effects on reproductive cells/organs and the need to discuss fertility preservation and family planning before starting treatment). While the relationship graph highlights impaired fertility directly, the entity description underscores that these considerations can be integral to selecting and sequencing therapies. [Data: Entities (869, 5704); Relationships (12771)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"The dataset balances risk with reassurance: healthy babies can still occur despite cancer and treatment\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Alongside pregnancy-related warnings, the community includes a positive outcome concept—HEALTHY BABIES—indicating that many people still have healthy babies despite cancer and its treatment. This serves as an important counterpoint in patient counseling, distinguishing possibility from prohibition while acknowledging risk management needs. [Data: Entities (5706); Relationships (12772)]\\n\\nThis framing, in combination with explicit risk relationships (harm to unborn baby; pregnancy testing), suggests the community’s informational focus is not solely on danger but on informed planning and monitoring to support safe outcomes when possible. [Data: Entities (5706, 5703, 2468); Relation