TMENT as guidance for the period after completion. This represents an explicit lifecycle transition from active therapy to follow-up considerations. [Data: Entities (1241, 869); Relationships (1998)]\\n\\nIt also captures a contingency scenario—TREATMENT STOPPING WORKING—where the patient asks how effectiveness is assessed and what options exist if therapy loses effectiveness. This highlights that treatment management includes reassessment and potential changes in strategy, not just initial selection. [Data: Entities (6390, 869); Relationships (14614)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Pre-treatment dental work is identified as a preparatory step to reduce complications for certain therapies\",\n      \"explanation\": \"DENTAL WORK OR PROCEDURES are described as dental interventions (including restorative care or surgical procedures) that may be recommended before initiating certain cancer treatments to reduce the risk of treatment-related complications and help protect the jaw, teeth, and gums during and after therapy. [Data: Entities (1471)]\\n\\nA relationship links TREATMENTS to DENTAL WORK OR PROCEDURES, emphasizing timing (before starting some cancer treatments) as part of risk reduction and supportive preparation. [Data: Relationships (2387); Entities (1471)]\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"rating\": 6.5,\n  \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity is moderately high because the community centers on cancer treatment decisions with significant safety implications (pregnancy harm risk, fertility effects, and hepatitis virus reactivation) and critical planning points across the care lifecycle. [Data: Entities (869, 2430, 5703, 5704, 2468,+more); Relationships (12770, 12771, 4337, 4443, 14614,+more)]\"\n}", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 2154, "prompt_tokens": 3750, "total_tokens": 5904, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_